ggml : copy the experts of a large batch ahead, on a second stream

Phase 6 of docs/moe-offload-plan.md in EuLLM. When a MUL_MAT_ID's experts
are in host memory (--moe-cache, --cpu-moe, --n-cpu-moe) and the GPU
computes it, the scheduler copies them to the card first: it reads the
router's choice back to the host, which waits for the GPU to drain, then
queues the copy on the GPU's own stream, where nothing computes while it
runs. Reading a long prompt alternates the two for every expert tensor of
every layer.

With llama_context_params.moe_prefetch_slots set (2 to 8; EuLLM's
--moe-prefetch, 4 by default), a MUL_MAT_ID of at least 512 tokens reads
its experts from one of that many slots in VRAM, each the size of the
largest expert tensor, instead of from the compute buffer. A second
instance of the GPU backend, a stream of its own, fills the slots a whole
tensor at a time while the splits before them compute. Two events per
slot order a copy after the previous reader of its slot, and the reading
split after the copy; the host waits for neither. The slots are placed
before ggml-alloc runs, which leaves them where they are, at the same
addresses every time. The padding past a copy's end is cleared by the
kernels that read it (MMQ, MMVQ), as in any compute buffer. In ggml it is
ggml_backend_sched_set_moe_prefetch, which llama.cpp calls on every
scheduler it creates for the context. It is declared in a header of its
own, ggml-backend-prefetch.h, so that the sources of the backends, which
all include ggml-backend.h, are not rebuilt for it.

Off unless set. One GPU of the CUDA backend (built for CUDA, or for HIP as
"ROCm") and one copy of the graph: other backends hold
on to what an asynchronous copy or an event takes until the next full
synchronization (Vulkan a new event per record, Metal a copy of the host
data per copy). The experts must be in the GPU's pinned host buffer, where
a model loaded without mmap has them: from pageable memory a copy would
hold the host until its slot is free. The slots are made only if they
leave a twentieth of the VRAM, and at least 512 MiB, to what a compute
allocates as it goes (the CUDA pool, cuBLAS), which aborts when it finds no
room. Anything it needs and does not find turns it off with one line on
stderr and gives the slots back, and the scheduler copies as before.

Ported to llama.cpp b86d2f0, where PR #29887 is merged and the copy of a
MUL_MAT_ID's experts is a callback of llama_context (sched_copy_experts)
instead of code in the scheduler: the prefetch no longer touches the MoE
cache's own code, and a staged copy simply skips the callback, which reads
the router's choice back and waits for the GPU to drain. The experts of a
large batch the cache holds are not read from VRAM on this path (the
callback's copy_experts does it where the prefetch is off).

EuLLM patch on llama.cpp b11925.

